2.2. Preparing the File for Printing

Once the design is complete, the file needs to be prepared for printing. This involves converting the 3D model into a format which the 3D printer can understand. The most commonly used format is STL (stereolithography). An STL is a triangulated representation of a 3D model [21]. The other common format is the AMF (additive manufacturing) file format, which is also understood by the slicing software.

2.3. Slicing the Model

After the file is prepared, the 3D model needs to be sliced. Slicing is the process of dividing the 3D model into thin layers. This is performed by using the slicing software. The thickness of the layers depends on the resolution of the printer. The slicing of the model is a very important step next to the modeling. This is because all parameters for printing that determine the quality and strength of the printed product depend on the setting of the slicing software. Nowadays, various types of software have been used as slicing software for 3D printing [22–24]. Among those are Cura, PrusaSlicer, Slic3r, Simplify3D, OctoPrint, AstroPrint, and Netfabb. They have their advantages and shortcomings. Based on the type of the printer and quality as well as price, one can choose one of them.

The slicing step is also responsible for making support structures when necessary. Not only support but also other parameters, which are crucially important for the strength and quality of the product. The sample model and sliced bracket model in Figure 3 are prepared by the Netfabb software.

