Once the object is printed, it may need to be finished. This involves removing any support structures that were used during the printing process. The object may also need to be sanded or polished to give it a smooth finish. Overall, 3D printing is a complex process that requires careful planning and execution. However, with the right tools and knowledge, anyone can create amazing 3D objects from the comfort of their own home or office.

3. Additive Manufacturing Types Used in Aerospace Applications

Additive manufacturing has revolutionized the aerospace industry by providing new production options that are faster, more flexible, and more cost-effective than conventional manufacturing. Additive manufacturing has a wide range of applications in the aerospace industry, from prototyping to manufacturing production parts. Several types of additive manufacturing methods are used for aerospace applications, including fused deposition modeling (FDM), stereolithography (SLA), selective laser sintering (SLS), direct metal laser sintering (DMLS), and electron beam melting (EBM) [8, 9, 22, 26].

Each type of additive manufacturing has its own set of advantages and disadvantages, and engineers choose the most appropriate method based on the specific requirements of the part they want to produce. Additive manufacturing is rapidly becoming the go-to method for aerospace manufacturing as it offers the potential for reduced production costs, improved design flexibility, and faster production times.

3.1. Stereolithography (SLA)

Stereolithography is a type of additive manufacturing process that uses ultraviolet light to solidify layers of resin to create three-dimensional objects as shown in Figure 5. SLA is a popular prototyping technology that has made significant advancements in the aerospace industry [28–30]. It is widely used for producing various complex aerospace products, including engine components, aircraft structures, and space vehicles. With the precision and versatility that stereolithography provides, aerospace engineers can design intricate parts that fit the complicated and precisely engineered requirements of aerospace products [20, 24, 31].

